Sort once, then binary search - fix this per-query sorting disaster. Inline in slow_search.py:

def nearest(values, query):
    s = sorted(values)
    best = s[0]
    for v in s:
        if abs(v - query) < abs(best - query):
            best = v
    return best

Workload: 100,000 seeded floats, 100,000 queries. Requirement: a NearestFinder class that sorts once in __init__ and answers each query with bisect in O(log n); total time for all 100,000 queries under 400ms (timed test, best of 3). Correctness: agreement with the naive function on 2,000 random queries including exact hits, below-min, above-max, and midpoints where two values tie (define and document the tie rule to match the naive version's behavior exactly - study it first). cProfile evidence of the before/after difference saved to search_prof.txt. pytest green.

